Line 246: `- {from: "no proper future C2 extension", to: "this class", reason: "C2 is a strictly larger extension class, so C2-inextendibility is strictly weaker"}`

Correct: E_C2 ⊂ E_C0 (E_C2 is the smallest extension class), so C2-inextendibility ("no C2 extension") is a WEAKER statement than C0-inextendibility. Wait, let's think about this carefully. Extension sets: E_C0 ⊇ E_H2loc ⊇ E_{C^1,1} ⊇ E_C2. "No proper future C2 extension" means E_C2 = ∅. "This class" says no proper future C0 extension, i.e. E_C0 = ∅. Since E_C2 ⊆ E_C0, E_C0 = ∅ ⟹ E_C2 = ∅. So "no C0 extension" entails "no C2 extension" (C0-inextendibility is stronger). Conversely, "no C2 extension" does NOT entail "no C0 extension"; it is strictly weaker. The forbidden transfer is from "no proper future C2 extension" to "this class" (C0), which is correctly forbidden. The reason should say: "C2 extensions form a strictly smaller extension class (E_C2 ⊂ E_C0), so C2-inextendibility is strictly weaker than this class's conclusion." The current sentence says "C2 is a strictly larger extension class" — wrong: C2 extension class is smaller (fewer extensions: C2 ⊂ C0). Actually wait — "larger extension class" might mean "class of extensions with more regularity"? But as a set of extensions, C2 extensions are a subset of C0 extensions. The file's own chain says E_C2 is innermost/smallest. So the premise is inverted.

Hmm, subtle: one might say "the C2 class is a larger *regularity* class" but the context is "extension class" = set of extensions. So worker-053/066/017 all agree it's inverted. Correct repair: either "C2 extensions form a strictly smaller extension class..." or F2a wording "the converse containment is false" (line 247 style). Let me check F2a line.

